 dict(kind="issue", key="vcftools", id="VT2", why="every version: --relatedness2 counts each individual's heterozygous sites over all its called sites while the numerator uses only sites called in both, so with missing genotypes the KING-robust kinship is scaled down by the pairwise call rate: duplicates 0.40 instead of 0.50 and parent-offspring 0.19 instead of 0.24 at 20 % missing, across the cut-offs users apply; fix on the branch gives the KING values (PR card goes live once the fork exists)"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="scdblfinder", id="SD1", why="every release since 1.4.0: with clusters given, createDoublets returns the size-adjusted 25 % of the artificial doublets after the others while getArtificialDoublets attaches the origin labels in the input order, so about half the artificial doublets are labelled with another pair of clusters and scDblFinder.mostLikelyOrigin is right for 24 % of heterotypic doublets on a simulated capture (100 % with the order kept); doublet calls unchanged; also a wrong halving count and a crash when one pair is adjusted; fix on the branch, testthat 17/17 (PR card goes live once plger/scDblFinder is forked)"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="scdblfinder", id="SD2", why="1.8.0 to devel: clusters passed as a factor (a colData column, mockDoubletSCE()$cluster) are stored as integer codes in the results table while the origins use the level names, so metadata(sce)$scDblFinder.stats shows 0 observed doublets for every combination with NA FNR and difficulty, plotDoubletMap is empty, and scDblFinder.cluster holds 1, 2, 3 instead of the labels; character labels work; one-line fix on the branch (PR card goes live once the fork exists)"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="scikit-learn", id="SK1", why="every release since 1.5.0: PCA's covariance_eigh solver, which svd_solver='auto' selects for dense data with at most 1000 features and at least ten times as many samples, forms the covariance as X.T @ X - n * mean (x) mean and loses the variance of features with a common offset to cancellation: float32 values around 100 with unit spread give explained_variance_ 33 % off, around 1e3 87 % off (ratio 54 %); float64 values around 1e6 1.7 % off, 1e7 20 % (PC1 scores 3.5 %), 1e8 meaningless; the full solver (the auto choice up to 1.4) is exact throughout; batched centred accumulation with the same memory footprint verified (harness 62/62, new test fails on main, test_pca.py 388 passed on the patched install); no prior report. WRITE THE ISSUE IN YOUR OWN WORDS from the fact sheet (repository policy); the PR follows triage"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="scikit-learn", id="SK2", why="every version: NMF.reconstruction_err_ and MiniBatchNMF.reconstruction_err_ are documented as 'Frobenius norm ... or beta-divergence' but are sqrt(2 * D_beta) for every loss: 40.12 for a KL divergence of 804.56; docstring patch on the branch. The documentation template allows a direct PR instead of an issue; either way, your own words (repository policy)"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="numpy", id="NP3", why="2.3+ (hash path of np.unique): np.unique(a, equal_nan=False) on datetime64 merges the NaT values when no optional output is requested, while return_counts/index/inverse, float NaN and every 1.x keep them; the complex NaN representative is the first met, not the documented smallest (np.unique(c) and np.unique(c, return_counts=True)[0] differ); fix verified on a source build of main (4 new test cases fail before, 643 passed after); no prior report. WRITE THE ISSUE AND PR TEXT IN YOUR OWN WORDS (NumPy AI policy); disclose AI use in the PR"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="numpy", id="NP5", why="every 2.x release and main: np.select with an int8 choice and default=300 returns 44 silently (-129 -> 127; a Python int among the choices wraps too); 1.x promoted to int16; np.where had the same bug and raises OverflowError since 2.5 (gh-30803); no prior report. Your own words (NumPy AI policy)"),
 dict(kind="issue", key="limma", id="LI1", why="a Bioconductor support-site post, not a GitHub issue: arrayWeights(method='reml') with prior weights divides its convergence criterion by the gene count twice, so it stops after two iterations instead of five and the weights differ by up to 14.7 percent from the same model without weights (137 vs 183 DE genes with voomWithQualityWeights); devel's voomLmFit(sample.weights=TRUE) routes there by design, so send it before limma 4.0.0; search the support site for prior reports first"),
